From ac80b99f0c7789c33a7a45605d6b446fdb5f639e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:24:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] host_loop_lease: republish and expire .want,
 release() during a hand-off wait, fixed-length metadata

Follow-up to PR #2119 review 5889959799 (card t_01a1d49d43e5e19e):

1. `.want` is one slot. A waiter now rewrites its `.want` once per interval from `held()`
   while it waits: it refreshes `at`, or republishes the record when another wanter's
   (dead, expired or lower-priority) record replaced it. A replaced record no longer
   lets a lower-priority LOCK_NB poller keep the lease while the waiter waits silently.
2. `.want` now carries the wanter's `interval`, and a record whose `at` is older than
   3 x interval is ignored by holder and standbys (one WARNING per wanter). A live but
   wedged wanter (SIGSTOPped) no longer stalls the pass. A failed `_spawn` of the
   hand-off thread clears `_waiting` and the `.want` instead of raising out of `held()`.
3. `release()` during a pending hand-off wait clears `_waiting` and leaves the blocked
   fd to its thread, which unlocks and closes it on wake-up. A later `held()` on the same
   object competes again, as the docstring says (it was False until the old thread woke).
   The thread also closes a left-over fd on a flock error.
4. Holder metadata is space-padded to a 1024-byte block, so a shorter rewrite never leaves
   the old record's tail; lock-free readers retry a read that does not parse once.

Proof: 6 new tests in tests/hermes_cli/test_host_loop_lease.py (republish after an
overwritten .want, multi-process; stale .want expiry with one warning; fresh .want still
defers; spawn failure; release() during a pending wait, multi-process with a SIGSTOPped
holder; fixed-length metadata; torn read retried). RED: all 6 fail on bea26cbf11's module;
GREEN: 22/22 three runs, doctor suites + lease 236 passed.
